{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for Task 2g.1 checkpoint-backed resident restart at exact integrated head 332e683643fa1ac171cb07a0e37cc88f9307e0d8. Head and clean status verified before and after every mutation; probes deleted; resident-100x never touched. Gates green on the pristine tree: vet, go test, -race, build, and ui npm test 14/14. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I re-derived both blockers and the sharpest coverage gap myself before signing.\n\nStart with what is right, because the core instinct is sound and worth keeping. The checkpoint stores original actor-signed events and re-folds them rather than caching a projected answer, which is why forged actor signatures, substituted payload bytes and added attachments all fail, and why the whole hostile-input battery degrades honestly: absent and foreign signatures, a checkpoint with a parent, wrong marker, extra tree files, foreign genesis, wrong object format, wrong schema, wrong fold profile, depth disagreeing with the event count, non-JSON, empty, truncated, trailing and non-canonical JSON, unknown fields, and oversize all reach a cache miss and a full audit. Divergent and stale checkpoints both behave: a head on a discarded fork is rejected by scanAfter, and a true-ancestor head extends to exact agreement with the full audit. gs verify never consults the checkpoint and attach never fetches it, so the auditor's path is untouched. Task 2g's repair and the approved Task 2b behaviour both survive the integration, and Task 2b's path privacy still holds.\n\nF1, BLOCKER. The checkpoint's event set is never bound to the chain. I confirmed this structurally rather than take it: every store call in loadCheckpoint reads the checkpoint commit itself — its signature, parents, message, file list and blob — while each cached event gets only validateObjectID, a hex-shape check, and HashPayloadTree, which binds payload to intent. Nothing reads the named event commits from the repository, and nothing compares the event list against the chain. Contrast loadEvent on the audit path, whose own comment says full and descendant scans share every envelope, actor signature, target, trailer, tree and payload check, and which requires CommitTree(commit) to equal the intent's tree. That is what binds an intent to its commit. The checkpoint is a third path that binds payload to intent and nothing to the chain. The delegate executed four hostile checkpoints, each re-signed with the real sequencer key, and all four were accepted as confident answers rather than cache misses: dropping event 0 of a three-event chain reported depth 2; reordering events changed the fold order; swapping two commit IDs was accepted; and commit IDs naming commits absent from the repository entirely were accepted.\n\nThe consequence is worse than a wrong answer, and it is why this is a blocker rather than a should-fix. With event 0 dropped, a resident cold-starts with that event's idempotency key missing from its dedup projection, so replaying that exact request appends a second commit with the same key — and gs verify then fails permanently with \"duplicates idempotent event\". The log is append-only, so there is no repair. The cache does not merely misreport the record; it causes an unauditable record to be written. By the same hole, since the fold is a pure function of the event list, omitting a role-grant or role-revoke rewrites authority without touching a signature.\n\nI want to state the threat model fairly: forging this needs the sequencer key, and the resident signs its own checkpoints from an in-memory accumulator. No live drift bug was found across the seven accumulation sites. But the invariant is unenforced — writeCheckpoint checks only that the event count matches the depth, which is length, not identity or order — unmonitored, and self-perpetuating, because a truncated checkpoint is faithfully re-signed and republished on every cadence. gitseq's chain is auditable precisely so that nobody has to trust a sequencer's assertion about it, and this reintroduces exactly such an assertion, unchecked, with the network removed. The fix is cheap: after validateCheckpoint, call RevList once and require the commit count to equal depth plus one, the first to be genesis, and each subsequent commit to equal the corresponding stored event. That is one git process, measured at 16ms for 1,963 commits against roughly 79ms per commit for a full audit, and it closes truncation, reordering, fabrication, nonexistent commits and depth lies without verifying a single extra signature. The speedup survives intact.\n\nF2, BLOCKER. A nil-pointer panic with a checkpoint clobber on the stateless submit path, and I confirmed it by reading the two adjacent lines. kernel.go:338 computes loadPayload as `cache != nil \u0026\u0026 options.CheckpointProfile != \"\"`; kernel.go:342 then enters the checkpoint-write block on `err == nil \u0026\u0026 options.CheckpointProfile != \"\"` with no cache guard, and increments cache.checkpointWrites inside. Calling the exported kernel.Submit with a CheckpointProfile and no cache therefore panics. The ordering makes it worse than a crash: loadPayload is false, so scanHead returns events with no payload bytes, writeCheckpoint still passes its only length check and CASes the ref — replacing a good checkpoint with one whose payloads can never reconstruct their trees — and only then does the process die. Not reachable from a shipped binary today, so this is an exported-API landmine rather than a live crash. Guard on cache != nil, and derive loadPayload and write-eligibility from one predicate so they cannot disagree.\n\nF3, should fix. The 256 cadence is attempt-based: checkpointAttempt is assigned before and independently of the write result, so a failed write pushes the next attempt 256 events further out and repeated failure extends the restart tail without bound. Every writeCheckpoint site tests for nil and drops the error, both loadCheckpoint failure sites drop the reason into an unexported counter, and the counters have no accessor — so there is no operator-visible signal that checkpoints are failing. The docs are honest about the bound being relative to a successfully written checkpoint, so this is robustness and observability rather than a false claim. Related and measured: 453 bytes per event, with the whole blob rewritten every 256 events, which is roughly 250MB written over a 20,000-event log's life and quadratic in N; at around 580,000 events it crosses the 256MB cap and the feature stops working silently, per the above.\n\nF4, should fix, and this is the one I re-ran myself. Deleting the checkpoint's sequencer-signature verification leaves the entire Go suite green — I confirmed it with -count=1 across all twelve packages, and vet clean. That is the single most load-bearing guard in the feature. Twelve more guards are equally uncovered, including the depth count, the head-is-last-event check, the actor signature, target binding, payload ceiling, duplicate-commit and dedup-conflict checks, unknown fields, and the read and size limits. Eleven are genuinely covered. The branch ships three checkpoint-negative tests, which map onto three of the covered results.\n\nOn the two claims I said I would check rather than accept. The provenance hash HOLDS, and I measured it independently: checkpoint.go is byte-identical at 54dd6f2 and the integrated head by three methods — blob id 1f3e5fd7152aa77c42d04744a2dac94f8cdc0248 at both revisions, cmp equal at 10,639 bytes, and SHA-256 05822c7a70784f15d770370f6117b1e549a59ff6fff495a91ce679d969902e22 on both extracted files. The benchmark file is byte-identical too. But the accompanying claim that the later changes only integrate the orientation base and relocate metric reporting is FALSE: 54dd6f2 is not an ancestor of the integrated head but a pre-rebase sibling, and the real delta also adds Task 2g's per-submit VerifySSHCommit — an extra verify-commit process on the very path that generates the fixture — and deletes two benchmarks. The timed region is unchanged so the number remains applicable; the description of the delta is not.\n\nThe 400x figure passes the test that mattered, and I want that on the record because the sibling branch failed it. The benchmark hard-asserts that the checkpoint was used, that all 20,000 events projected and that full scans were zero, builds its events through the real signed submit path, reads the real blob back, and discloses the tail — and the two benchmarks using the sibling's fabricated pattern were deleted in this integration. So that defect is genuinely fixed here. What the figure omits is that the ratio is governed almost entirely by where the tail lands, which it does not state: restart cost is roughly tail times 79ms, cold is N times 79ms, and refreshes land on multiples of 256, so a re-measurement at depth 1,000 landed on tail 232 and gave 4.1x while depth 20,000 landed on tail 32 and gives a few hundred x. Same code, same machine, a hundredfold swing from an unstated variable. Quote the figure with its tail. The cadence bound itself held at both depths.\n\nF1 is one rev-list away from closed, and with F2's guard and tests for the uncovered checkpoint guards this is approvable."}